      en: "The Union Buildings in Pretoria, South Africa, are the official seat of the South African government and the president. The imposing neoclassical building was completed in 1913 and designed by architect Herbert Baker. It sits on Meintjieskop, a hill overlooking the city. The Union Buildings have been the site of key national events, including Nelson Mandela's inauguration in 1994. The building is surrounded by extensive gardens open to the public. It symbolizes the unity of the nation and is a major landmark of the capital.",
